WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:04.350
I want to talk about Ukraine today, but this
is extremely complicated and I just want to

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.250
apologize in advance for anything that I get
wrong / leave out / over-simplify.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.250
Okay?

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.250
Okay!

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.269
So this is Ukraine, the second largest country
in Europe by area.

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:15.409
And this is the Crimean Peninsula, which saw
much of the fighting in the Crimean War that

00:00:15.409 --> 00:00:17.130
lasted from 1853 to 1856.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.470
It was sort of a 'Catholic versus Orthodox'
thing, but the larger cause was Europe fearing

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.130
that Russia's power would expand as the Ottoman
empire declined.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.949
So Britain, France, and the kinda-still-a-thing
Ottoman Empire teamed up to eventually defeat

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.960
Russia and five hundred thousand people died,
and it was generally pretty awful, and obviously

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.489
resolved very little since Europe - just sixty
years later - would go ahead and have World

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:41.219
War I. Then towards the end of World War I,
Russia had its famous communist revolution

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.280
and Ukraine enjoyed a brief period of independence.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.870
Although 'enjoyed' may not be quite the right
verb.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:52.180
In fact from 1917 to 1921 Ukraine was mired
in endless wars among competing factions including

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.350
Poland and the USSR and Ukrainian Nationalists,
even some real live anarchists.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.170
It was all very complicated.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.120
It was also extremely violent.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.129
More than 1.5 million people died.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:07.500
And then finally Ukraine became part of the
USSR, and things became more stable for a

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.000
while, but then...

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Stalin.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.740
As many as 10 million Ukrainians starved to
death in 1932 and 1933 thanks to Stalin's

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:18.720
agricultural policies, which were not just
stupid but also actively evil.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.659
I mean, there was widespread starvation in
a country that is now the world's third largest

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:24.280
exporter of grain.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.000
Then following World War II, Stalin forcibly
deported the Crimean peninsula's entire indigenous

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.430
population of ethnic Tatars to central Asia.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.340
So if you wanna point to one individual who's
responsible for a lot of this, I don't wanna,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.430
you know, call anyone out, but STALIN.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:41.390
Stalin also moved many ethnic Russians into
Ukraine, especially into the Crimean peninsula

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.700
in the western part of the country, which
is the industrial center.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.930
And then Stalin's successor, Khrushchev, decided
to transfer the Crimean peninsula from Russia

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.770
to Ukraine in 1954.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:52.770
Why?

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.450
Well, Khrushchev had lots of ties to Ukraine,
but also the Crimean peninsula is not actually

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.320
physically attached to Russia.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.250
As you can see here, it is physically attached
to Ukraine and it gets all of its electricity

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:07.020
and water from Ukraine, and it's easier to
administer places you are physically connected

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.020
to.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.060
Which is why Alaska should be part of Canada,
but that is a different story.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:16.000
Okay, so quick contemporary demographic snapshot:
today, about 67% of people living in Ukraine

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.000
speak Ukrainian as their first language.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.290
About 30% speak Russian, although only about
17% of the population identify as ethnically

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:24.290
Russian.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.321
And as you can see here Russian is mostly
spoken in the West (he meant East) and in

00:02:26.321 --> 00:02:27.370
the Crimean peninsula.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.780
Many Tatars have returned to Crimea since
the breakup of the Soviet Union, but today

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.250
they only make up about 12% of the population.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:38.080
Okay, so flash forward to December of 1991
after the Soviet Union has broken up: Ukraine

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.030
has had a nationwide referendum and 90% of
people, including a majority of those living

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.690
in the Crimean peninsula, voted for independence
from Russia.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:50.810
But Ukraine remained much more closely aligned
with Russia than many other former Soviet

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.700
Republics did like, for instance, Estonia,
which is now part of the European Union.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.900
In 2004 there was an election and there were
widespread reports of vote-rigging, but the

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.730
Russian-friendly Viktor Yanukovych was elected.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.230
The opposition leader, Viktor Yushchenko,
lead massive street protests in Kiev that

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.700
came to be known as the Orange Revolution.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.190
That opposition leader, Yushchenko, was disfigured
and almost died as a result of mysterious

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.190
poisoning.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.890
Side note, but the number of "mysterious poisonings"
in Russia and Eastern Europe has absolutely

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.930
skyrocketed since Putin came to power in Russia,
which I'm sure is a coincidence.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:27.630
Anyway, the Orange Revolution protests led
to a second election, and the poisoned opposition

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:32.590
leader, Yushchenko, won, and yay, everything
would be made of puppies and freedom and rainbows

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.870
and friendliness toward Europe forever, except
NO.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.230
For one thing, a lot of people in Ukraine,
especially in the western part, want to be

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:43.780
more closely allied with Russia, and also
(despite being an economist) Yushchenko wasn't

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.200
very good at running the Ukrainian economy.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.970
Also, Yushchenko couldn't push through austerity
measures needed to deal with Ukraine's rising

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:54.480
debt, and his friendliness towards Europe
infuriated Russia, which cut off gas supplies

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.690
briefly (but disastrously) to Ukraine in 2006.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.860
So, by 2010, Ukraine was being led by the
Europe friendly, and somewhat corrupt, Yulia

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:05.160
Tymoshenko and then there were elections.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.590
And the presidential election, declared free
and fair by international observers, was won

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.180
by the aforementioned, Russian-friendly, Viktor
Yanukovych.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:18.320
In November 2013, Yanukovych announced that
Ukraine would abandon an agreement to strengthen

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.970
ties with the EU and would instead become
a closer ally of Russia.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.120
And that is when the protests began in Kiev's
Independence Square.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.340
Those protests grew and grew until February
20th when dozens of protesters were killed

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:36.410
by military and police and the next day Yanukovych
disappeared from Kiev.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.250
The protesters had won.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.310
They installed a new temporary government
to prepare for new elections... and then the

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.260
Putin regime marched into the Crimean Peninsula,
ostensibly to protect ethnic Russians there

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:52.380
and Russian military installations, but this
violation of Ukraine's territorial integrity

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.920
is known in diplomatic circles as, like, "a
big 'effing deal."

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:00.760
Crimea is already an autonomous province with
its own government, so what does it want?

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.030
Does it want independence?

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:06.180
That would be hard, all of their electricity
and water has to come through Ukraine.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.030
Does it want to be part of Russia again?

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:12.870
That would be impossible, without Ukrainian
co-operation, which right now seems inconceivable,

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.060
so that's where we are right now.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.450
I mean, unless something has happened in the
last 30 minutes.

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:21.120
So here's one narrative of these events: an
unpopular and ineffective, but democratically

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.440
elected politician was removed from power
by a mob of protesters, and the new, unelected

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.910
parliament briefly passed a law saying that
only Ukrainian can be the official language

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.210
of the country, even though many people in
the country speak Russian.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.720
Furthermore, this new government wants to
become part of the EU which might bring NATO

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.170
missiles to Russia's border and that is unacceptable
to Russia.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:42.610
I mean, I'm American.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.130
We've had some very infective and unpopular
leaders, and what we've done is just waited

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.130
for them not to be president anymore.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:53.490
But here's another narrative: a tyrannical
leader who ordered the murder of peaceful

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:58.770
protesters was chased from power and replaced
by a government that will transition Ukraine

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:03.980
toward free and fair elections and Russia
responded to that by invading Ukraine.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.281
Hank, I'm not trying to make a false equivalence
here, but I think it's really important to

00:06:07.281 --> 00:06:08.430
understand both of these narratives.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.310
And I wanted to give a little more historical
context than we've been seeing on the news

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:15.230
because it helps us to understand that the
pull between Western Europe and Russia in

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.840
Ukraine is not new.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:21.430
I mean, the word "Ukraine" itself means "borderland".

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:25.300
It has for centuries functioned as the border
between West and East.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.750
What Ukraine needs is stability...

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:31.830
decades of stability, so it can grow to have
its own identity to have relationships with

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.740
both Europe and Russia without being controlled
by either.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:41.060
Right now that dream seems really far away
but 60 years ago Japan was in ruins. 25 years

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:43.590
ago Germany was divided into two countries.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:49.780
12 years ago Sierra Leone was mired in a horrific
and seemingly endless civil war but today

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.280
it's the second fastest growing economy in
the world.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.010
The arc of history is long, Hank, let's hope
that it bends towards peace.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.050
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

